<!DOCTYPE template>
<!--
  The four waveforms, decks 1|2 over 3|4.

  The two rows have deliberately different vertical policies, and that is what
  makes an unused pair cost nothing. The bottom row is `max` with no <Size>,
  so it is exactly as tall as its visible cells and collapses to nothing when
  both are hidden. The top row is `me`, so it expands into whatever the bottom
  row does not take.

  That is also the whole mechanism behind the 60/40 split. There is no stretch
  factor in this skin system at all: WWidgetGroup::addWidget calls
  QLayout::addWidget with no stretch argument, and setupSize parses only Size,
  SizePolicy, MinimumSize and MaximumSize. So the one number that decides how
  the two rows divide the band is the bottom cells' minimum height, and the top
  row takes whatever is left. Each row's cells bind minimumHeight to their own
  control (below), seeded by skin.xml's manifest and, for the bottom row,
  written by the Split row on Settings -> General.

  Measured on the panel at 1280x720, the band is 355px: y=45 to y=399, under
  the topbar and over the library band's top border. At the shipped default of
  top 178, bottom 142:

    four decks   top 213, bottom 142   the 60/40 split
    two decks    top 355, bottom 0     full height for the pair in use
    three decks  top 213, bottom 142   deck 3 spans the full width

  The other three Split settings measure top 177/195/230 against bottom
  178/160/125, all within 0.2 points of the ratio on their label.

  Deck 3 alone in the bottom row gets the 40% height, not half the band. There
  is no way to condition a height on how many decks are loaded, for the same
  reason the next paragraph gives about visibility.

  Binding a row's visibility to "either of its decks is loaded" is not
  expressible here (one Connection carries one ConfigKey, and two of them
  fighting over `visible` breaks the case that matters), so the collapse is
  done with size policy instead. Do not "simplify" both rows to the same
  policy: two MinimumExpanding rows split the height evenly whether or not
  either holds anything, which is the reserved-black bug in another costume.
-->
<Template>
  <WidgetGroup>
    <ObjectName>Waveforms</ObjectName>
    <Layout>vertical</Layout>
    <SizePolicy>me,me</SizePolicy>
    <Children>

      <WidgetGroup>
        <ObjectName>WaveformRowTop</ObjectName>
        <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
        <SizePolicy>me,me</SizePolicy>
        <Children>
          <Template src="skin:waveform.xml">
            <SetVariable name="channel">1</SetVariable>
            <SetVariable name="minHeightKey">[BiteDJ],waveform_min_top</SetVariable>
          </Template>
          <Template src="skin:waveform.xml">
            <SetVariable name="channel">2</SetVariable>
            <SetVariable name="minHeightKey">[BiteDJ],waveform_min_top</SetVariable>
          </Template>
        </Children>
      </WidgetGroup>

      <WidgetGroup>
        <ObjectName>WaveformRowBottom</ObjectName>
        <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
        <SizePolicy>me,max</SizePolicy>
        <Children>
          <Template src="skin:waveform.xml">
            <SetVariable name="channel">3</SetVariable>
            <SetVariable name="minHeightKey">[BiteDJ],waveform_min_bottom</SetVariable>
          </Template>
          <Template src="skin:waveform.xml">
            <SetVariable name="channel">4</SetVariable>
            <SetVariable name="minHeightKey">[BiteDJ],waveform_min_bottom</SetVariable>
          </Template>
        </Children>
      </WidgetGroup>

    </Children>
  </WidgetGroup>
</Template>
